# Line endings are pinned here, not left to whoever cloned the repository.
#
# This is not tidiness. Without this file, a clone with `core.autocrlf=true`
# checks every text file out as CRLF -- and `bin/mc-art` has NO EXTENSION, so no
# `*.sh` pattern would have covered it. A CRLF shebang script does not run:
#
#     $ bin/mc-art --help
#     bash: bin/mc-art: /usr/bin/env: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#     bin/mc-art: line 1: $'\r': command not found
#
# and the broken copy is vendored straight into the npm package by the panel
# build, so `npm install` hands people a POSIX launcher that cannot execute.
# Measured before this file existed: bin/mc-art was 2020 B with 53 CR in an
# autocrlf=true clone, against 1967 B with 0 CR here.
#
# The rule is about what each platform's own tooling expects, not about taste:
# POSIX shells and Python reject CRLF; cmd.exe and PowerShell are happiest with
# it. `scripts/check-platform.py` asserts both, and `git check-attr` is what it
# asks -- a rule that exists in this file but does not take effect is the same
# bug in a new costume.

# --- the entry points -------------------------------------------------------
# No extension, so it must be named in full: *.sh would not match it.
bin/mc-art        text eol=lf
*.sh              text eol=lf
# Windows launchers: CRLF is what cmd.exe and PowerShell expect.
*.cmd             text eol=crlf
*.bat             text eol=crlf
*.ps1             text eol=crlf

# --- source and docs --------------------------------------------------------
*.py              text eol=lf
*.md              text eol=lf
*.json            text eol=lf
*.js              text eol=lf
*.mjs             text eol=lf
*.ts              text eol=lf
*.txt             text eol=lf
*.yml             text eol=lf
*.yaml            text eol=lf
.gitattributes    text eol=lf
.gitignore        text eol=lf

# --- never touched ----------------------------------------------------------
# Assets must survive a checkout byte for byte: a jar whose bytes changed is a
# different jar, and a PNG whose bytes changed has different pixels.
*.png             binary
*.jpg             binary
*.jpeg            binary
*.gif             binary
*.jar             binary
*.nbt             binary
*.pyc             binary
